{
  "id": "COMPOUND:31405 - PROTEIN:5848",
  "verbalization": "Alstoyunine H is consistently linked to the cancer cell line Raji through multiple independent pathways, all of which converge on Caspase-5 as a key target. The evidence suggests that Alstoyunine H modulates Caspase-5, an enzyme critical in the regulation of apoptosis, and this modulation is directed toward Raji cells. The diverse chemical structures of the targets—such as [(1S,2R,4S,5R,8R,10S,13R,14R,17S,18R)-2-hydroxy-4,5,9,9,13,20,20-heptamethyl-24-oxahexacyclo[15.5.2.01,18.04,17.05,14.08,13]tetracosan-10-yl] acetate, 5-Oxobrucine, and [1-(4,8-Dimethoxy-1-methyl-2-oxoquinolin-3-yl)-3-methylbut-3-en-2-yl] hexadecanoate—indicate that Alstoyunine H may act through a variety of mechanisms, potentially involving different modes of action that ultimately target Caspase-5. The consistent targeting of Raji cells across these pathways supports the hypothesis that Alstoyunine H exerts its effects through a mechanism that involves Caspase-5, likely by inducing apoptosis in Raji cells. The evidence suggests that the relationship between Alstoyunine H and Raji is grounded in a well-defined, mechanistically plausible pathway involving Caspase-5, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
